WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.960
Speaker 1: . I received a ton of questions this&nbsp;
week since our coverage Monday about Israeli Prime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:11.360
Minister Benjamin Netanyahu being out and Naftali&nbsp;
Bennett being in as prime minister, and the gist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:15.680
of the question is, will this actually make a&nbsp;
difference in terms of the Israeli-Palestinian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:21.440
conflict than the answer is we just don't&nbsp;
know yet. There are folks who are claiming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:26.000
that there is absolutely no difference between&nbsp;
Naftali Bennett and Benjamin Netanyahu on their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:31.840
willingness to make change to the status quo when&nbsp;
it comes to the Israeli-Palestinian conflict. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:37.200
I think that there's some truth. There's truth to&nbsp;
the reality that Naftali Bennett is still a right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:43.200
winger. There's no question about that, but not&nbsp;
from the party, Likud and a different approach.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:47.280
And what you have to understand is that a&nbsp;
lot of what progress can be made comes down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:51.600
to the coalition. The coalition, which&nbsp;
includes members of multiple parties,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:56.160
agrees on some things. And that's typically&nbsp;
where the vast majority of the focuses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:03.120
and on areas where they disagree, nothing tends&nbsp;
to happen. But the other side to this is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:10.080
no matter who you put in power in Israel,&nbsp;
if there is not some kind of coherent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:14.240
change, Choko coherent and productive change&nbsp;
to Palestinian leadership, nothing will happen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:21.600
There's just no chance whatsoever. I've been&nbsp;
saying for years a prerequisite for change would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:26.800
be Benjamin Netanyahu not being prime minister.&nbsp;
We've achieved that. OK, we've got someone who's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:32.080
slightly less right wing now and might be&nbsp;
willing to make some changes and negotiate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:36.000
in some areas. But on the other hand,&nbsp;
you still have Gaza controlled by Hamas.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:42.000
You have a Palestinian Authority in the West Bank&nbsp;
that while certainly more willing to work with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.640
Israel and there is a, you know, worker program&nbsp;
and there's much more happening than with Gaza&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:58.160
is at odds in many ways with Hamas and completely&nbsp;
unwilling Hamas, completely unwilling to cede&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:03.360
power to maybe allow the Palestinian Authority to&nbsp;
negotiate on everyone's behalf. I mean, it's like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:08.480
I sound crazy even talking about this stuff&nbsp;
because there's zero chance that this is going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:12.240
to happen. So I don't know the answer.&nbsp;
And as I mentioned earlier in the week,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:17.360
the vast majority of the Israeli population&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:28.080
is concerned with and prioritizing domestic issues&nbsp;
like economics and wages and infrastructure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:34.240
much like we do in the United States.&nbsp;
And so particularly as outsiders, when we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:41.760
look at, you know, a place like Ukraine. And&nbsp;
for us, one of the big issues we associate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:47.520
with Ukraine is the conflict with Russia and&nbsp;
Putin versus Ukraine. And all of this stuff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:53.520
for the vast majority of Ukrainians actually have&nbsp;
a Ukrainian friend who was just just there. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:57.680
said, how much do people talk about? Putin&nbsp;
says, you know, it's casual conversation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:03.040
but the vast majority of it is there's roads that&nbsp;
are a disaster in Ukraine. There's roads that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:10.080
so potholed that it took four hours to go what on&nbsp;
a normal road would take half an hour in order to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:16.160
get to the village that he was originally from.&nbsp;
That's what people there care about. And this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:20.640
one of the great distortions. You know, people&nbsp;
talk about the distortion of following foreign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:29.360
policy, of, well, you take on the biases of&nbsp;
whatever the media in your country has. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:37.120
much of the American left likes to say almost all&nbsp;
American media is biased pro Israel. The real bias&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:42.880
is that we end up thinking that the big issues&nbsp;
domestically are the Israeli-Palestinian conflict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:48.560
when they are not. And similarly, based on what&nbsp;
we hear about Ukraine, we might think everybody's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:54.080
constantly talking about the situation with Putin.&nbsp;
Another example, I've spoken to Korean friends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.080 --> 00:04:00.480
who are they live in the United States but&nbsp;
have family in South Korea. And, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:05.760
at a moment when there was a big flare up with&nbsp;
missile launches from Kim Jong Il and I said, oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:11.200
how's your family thinking about or dealing with&nbsp;
the rocket launches? And he said, you know, it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:16.560
it really if you're right there and have reason&nbsp;
to think that you may be in danger, it's something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:23.760
that might be on your radar, so to speak. But&nbsp;
for his family, it wasn't really an issue. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:29.520
jobs. It was health care. It was globalization&nbsp;
and and so many other things. And so the real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:35.200
cognitive distortion that happens as Americans&nbsp;
following the politics of other countries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:39.760
as we tend to focus only on the things that are&nbsp;
externally facing and maybe relate to us in some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:46.240
way, which is logical because, hey, we're us. And&nbsp;
that's in some sense what we care about. But the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:51.280
internal situation is often much different.&nbsp;
So needless to say, I think that there is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:59.200
Five to seven percent greater possibility that&nbsp;
we start to see some serious negotiations when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:04.080
it comes to the Israeli-Palestinian conflict, but&nbsp;
if and until there's a leadership change in Gaza,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:09.840
I just don't see how anything is going to change.&nbsp;
If you have a different view, let me know.

